    {
      "id": "word-negamos--refuse",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "negamos",
      "answerEn": "we refuse",
      "noteEn": "Negamos by itself usually means “we deny.” With nos before it and a before a following action, nos negamos a expresses that a group including the speaker refuses to do that action.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Nos negamos a mentir.",
        "en": "We refuse to lie."
      },
      "tags": ["word", "negarse", "present", "first person plural", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-a--negarse-complement",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "a",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; required after negarse before the refused action",
      "noteEn": "When negarse means “to refuse,” Spanish uses a to connect it to the action that someone will not do.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Se niegan a esperar.",
        "en": "They refuse to wait."
      },
      "tags": ["word", "preposition", "negarse", "verb complement", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-negarse-a-infinitive",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "negarse a hacer algo",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone refuses to do something?",
      "answerEn": "Use the matching form of negarse, then a, then the action verb in its unchanged dictionary form.",
      "noteEn": "The short marker and the form of negar both match the person: me niego means “I refuse,” se niega means “he, she, or you formally refuse,” and nos negamos means “we refuse.” A comes next. The refused action stays in its dictionary form, the unchanged form ending in -ar, -er, or -ir, as in salir.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Lucía se niega a salir.",
        "en": "Lucía refuses to leave."
      },
      "tags": ["grammar", "negarse", "refusal", "verb pattern", "A2"]
    },
    {
